    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stonehill/jobs/5457515/advancement-gift-entry-specialist": {
      "title": "Advancement Gift Entry Specialist",
      "description": "Advancement Gift Entry Specialist Salary $40,040.00 - $45,500.00 Annually Location Easton, MA Job Type Hourly Benefit Eligible Job Number 00003 Department Advancement-Fundraising Salaries Opening Date 08/21/2026 FLSA Non-Exempt Bargaining Unit N/A Description Benefits Founded by the Congregation of Holy Cross in 1948, Stonehill is a private Catholic college located just 22 miles from downtown Boston on a beautiful 384-acre campus in Easton, Massachusetts. With a student-to-faculty ratio of 13:1, the College engages its 2,500+ students in 80+ rigorous academic programs in the liberal arts, sciences, and pre-professional fields. Faithful to the Holy Cross tradition in education, Stonehill College is committed to developing the moral, spiritual, intellectual, physical, and social competencies of its students and seeks to build and sustain a campus community that embraces diversity and inclusion in its teaching, learning, living, and working. Stonehill values a diversity of persons, opinions, and cultural and religious perspectives. In fulfillment of its motto, Lux et Spes (“Light and Hope”), the College cultivates in its students the competence to think, act, and lead with courage towards creating a more just and compassionate world. Description This position will be responsible for the day-to-day gift processing, constituent demographic data updates, and reporting, ensuring timely and accurate data entry, gift processing, acknowledgement, and reporting. Responsibilities Primarily responsible for entering charitable gift data for the College into the Advancement database system. Oversees aspects of the matching gift processing, including gift entry and donation confirmation, as well as usernames & passwords for matching gift companies’ confirmations. Interacts with the Controller’s Office to ensure proper gift processing. Prepares gift acknowledgement/receipt correspondence. Prepares and generates all donor thank-you letters & tax acknowledgments daily. Tracks and processes In Memory Of and In Honor Of acknowledgments and reporting. Scans gift materials into the Advancement database system using document imaging functionality. Enters and maintains constituent information and makes necessary updates in the Advancement database to ensure data accuracy and integrity. Respond courteously and professionally to donor inquiries via phone or email. Supports reporting processes for mailings, events, and donor giving for internal and community partners’ use. Maintains the Development Office Retention schedule and follows established retention plans. Aids in data projects and performs related duties as assigned by the Director of Advancement Operations. Other duties as assigned. Typical Qualifications Associate’s degree (A.A.) or equivalent from a two-year college or technical school; or six months to two years’ related experience and/or training; or equivalent of education and experience. Supplemental Information To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to satisfy each essential duty. The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Language Skills Oral Communication – Speaks clearly and persuasively in positive or negative situations; Listens and gets clarification; Responds well to questions. Written Communication – Writes clearly and informatively; Edits work for spelling and grammar; Able to read and interpret written information. Computer Skills Document Preparation – Proficient in the use of Microsoft Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, and web browsers. Data Entry – Experience with Banner/Raiser’s Edge preferred; matching gift companies, 3rd Party companies like Benevity,NPO, Front Door, etc. Interpersonal Skills Customer Service – Manages difficult or emotional customer situations; Responds promptly to customer needs. Responds to requests for service and assistance; Meets commitments. Discretion – Maintains confidentiality; Listens to others without interrupting. Professionalism – Tactfully approaches others; Reacts well under pressure; Treats others with respect and consideration regardless of their status or position; Accepts responsibility for own actions; Follows through on commitments. Adaptability – Adapts to changes in the work environment. Able to deal with frequent change, delays, or unexpected events. Well-organized and flexible with the ability to work independently in a fast-paced environment. Organizational Skills Organizational Support – Follows policies and procedures; Completes administrative tasks correctly and on time; Supports the organization’s goals and values; Ability to multitask and independently prioritize competing tasks and deadlines. Attendance/Punctuality – Is consistently at work and on time. Dependability – Follows instructions, responds to management direction. Stonehill College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees, based on position, which includes: Medical, prescription drug, vision, and dental coverage Paid vacation, holidays, and various leave programs Competitive retirement benefits, including defined contribution plans and voluntary tax-deferred savings options Employee and dependent educational benefits Life insurance coverage Employee discount programs Voluntray benefits For detailed information on benefits and eligibility, please visit here for more information. Employer Stonehill College (MA) Address 320 Washington Street Easton, Massachusetts, 02357 Phone 508-565-1105 Website www.stonehill.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stonehill/jobs/5463086/assistant-director-of-advancement-communications": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Advancement Communications",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Advancement Communications Salary $69,000.00 - $74,000.00 Annually Location Easton, MA Job Type Admin Benefit Eligible Job Number 00029 Department Advancement Communications Salaries Opening Date 08/27/2026 FLSA Exempt Bargaining Unit N/A Description Benefits Founded by the Congregation of Holy Cross in 1948, Stonehill is a private Catholic college located just 22 miles from downtown Boston on a beautiful 384-acre campus in Easton, Massachusetts. With a student-to-faculty ratio of 13:1, the College engages its 2,500+ students in 80+ rigorous academic programs in the liberal arts, sciences, and pre-professional fields. Faithful to the Holy Cross tradition in education, Stonehill College is committed to developing the moral, spiritual, intellectual, physical, and social competencies of its students and seeks to build and sustain a campus community that embraces diversity and inclusion in its teaching, learning, living, and working. Stonehill values a diversity of persons, opinions, and cultural and religious perspectives. In fulfillment of its motto, Lux et Spes (“Light and Hope”), the College cultivates in its students the competence to think, act, and lead with courage towards creating a more just and compassionate world. Description The Assistant Director of Advancement Communications is a key member of the Advancement Communications team, responsible for supporting the development and implementation of strategic communication plans that strengthen engagement with alumni, donors, parents, the campus community, and other key stakeholders. This role involves creating compelling content for a variety of channels, including print, digital, and social media, and working collaboratively across the Advancement Division and with campus partners to support and promote fundraising and alumni engagement initiatives while elevating the College’s brand and advancing it mission and vision. Responsibilities Writing, Content Creation, and Strategy - Collaborate with the Director of Advancement Communications to develop and execute a comprehensive communications strategy for alumni and donor engagement. - Collect and create compelling stories that highlight the impact of alumni connection, donor gifts, student success, and the transformative power of philanthropy. - Write, edit, and produce high-quality content for a variety of platforms, including the monthly alumni e-newsletter, the Stonehill Alumni Magazine (print and digital editions), the alumni social media channels, websites, and email campaigns. - This position will work as the associate editor of the Stonehill Alumni Magazine, brainstorming and researching story ideas, conducting interviews, writing stories and class notes, working with freelance writers, coordinating photography, sharing design input, proofing, and managing media releases and the approval process. - Assist with tracking and analyzing the effectiveness of communications campaigns. - Ensure consistent and effective messaging that aligns with Stonehill College’s mission and values, while reinforcing the institution’s brand identity across all Advancement Communications materials. Fundraising and Campaign Support - Assist with the promotion of fundraising initiatives, such as campaigns, annual giving, and special giving days. - Support the development of fundraising materials, including case statements, social media posts, profiles and stories, and digital assets. Social Media & Digital Presence - Assist with developing strategies to engage with alumni, donors, and other key constituencies on digital platforms and increase visibility and engagement with Stonehill’s Advancement efforts. - Assist in promoting alumni/donor-focused events through email and social media channels. Collaborative Teamwork - Work closely with colleagues in Advancement, Marketing, Communications and Media Relations to coordinate communication and ensure message alignment. - Collaborate with campus partners to gather stories, data, and other materials that support Advancement goals. Event Coverage and Support - Attend alumni and donor events, such as Reunion and Homecoming, to cover for recaps and social media posts and to accompany freelance photographers. - Support events within the Advancement Division as needed; occasional weekends. Typical Qualifications - Bachelor's degree in Communications, Marketing, or related field and 3-5 years of experience in communications, marketing, or advancement, preferably in higher education or nonprofit sectors. - Exceptional writing, editing, and proofreading skills, with an ability to craft clear, compelling, and persuasive messages for a variety of audiences and platforms. - Strong attention to detail and ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously and handle multiple deadlines. - Experience with email marketing software, social media management, and content management systems (CMS), and knowledge of analytics tools and ability to interpret data to improve strategies. Supplemental Information To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Language Skills - Oral Communication - Speaks clearly and persuasively in positive or negative situations; Listens and gets clarification; Responds well to questions. - Written Communication - Writes clearly and informatively; Edits work for spelling and grammar; Able to read and interpret written information. Computer Skills - Document Preparation - Proficient in the use of Microsoft Word, Excel, Outlook, Adobe Creative Suite, and Canva. - Data Entry - Experience with Raiser’s Edge preferred. Interpersonal Skills - Customer Service - Manages difficult or emotional constituent situations; Responds promptly to constituent needs. Responds to requests for service and assistance; Meets commitments. - Discretion - Maintains confidentiality; Listens to others without interrupting. - Professionalism - Tactfully approaches others; Reacts well under pressure; Treats others with respect and consideration regardless of their status or position; Accepts responsibility for own actions; Follows through on commitments. - Adaptability - Adapts to changes in the work environment. Able to deal with frequent change, delays, or unexpected events. Well-organized and flexible with the ability to work independently in a fast-paced environment. Organizational Skills - Organizational Support - Follows policies and procedures; Completes administrative tasks correctly and on time; supports the organization's goals and values; Ability to multitask and independently prioritize competing tasks and deadlines. - Attendance/Punctuality - Is consistently at work and on time. - Dependability - Follows instructions, responds to management direction. Stonehill College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees, based on position, which includes: Medical, prescription drug, vision, and dental coverage Paid vacation, holidays, and various leave programs Competitive retirement benefits, including defined contribution plans and voluntary tax-deferred savings options Employee and dependent educational benefits Life insurance coverage Employee discount programs Voluntray benefits For detailed information on benefits and eligibility, please visit here for more information. Employer Stonehill College (MA) Address 320 Washington Street Easton, Massachusetts, 02357 Phone 508-565-1105 Website www.stonehill.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stonehill/jobs/5491096/assistant-director-of-conference-event-services": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Conference & Event Services",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Conference & Event Services Salary $60,000.00 - $65,000.00 Annually Location Easton, MA Job Type Admin Benefit Eligible Job Number 00050 Department Conference & Events Salaries Opening Date 09/22/2026 FLSA Exempt Bargaining Unit N/A Description Benefits Founded by the Congregation of Holy Cross in 1948, Stonehill is a private Catholic college located just 22 miles from downtown Boston on a beautiful 384-acre campus in Easton, Massachusetts. With a student-to-faculty ratio of 13:1, the College engages its 2,500+ students in 80+ rigorous academic programs in the liberal arts, sciences, and pre-professional fields. Faithful to the Holy Cross tradition in education, Stonehill College is committed to developing the moral, spiritual, intellectual, physical, and social competencies of its students and seeks to build and sustain a campus community that embraces diversity and inclusion in its teaching, learning, living, and working. Stonehill values a diversity of persons, opinions, and cultural and religious perspectives. In fulfillment of its motto, Lux et Spes (“Light and Hope”), the College cultivates in its students the competence to think, act, and lead with courage towards creating a more just and compassionate world. Description The Assistant Director of Conference & Event Services will contribute to the department’s efforts in maintaining a high level of customer service and support to both internal and external campus constituencies and in maximizing revenue through the utilization of campus facilities by external campus constituencies. The Assistant Director will provide oversight to the scheduling of all campus facilities and the distribution of event information to campus stakeholders and support staff departments. In addition, the Assistant Director will provide support to the Associate Director in the recruitment of new Conference and Events business and in preserving strong relationships with the department’s existing client base. Fostering an atmosphere of collaboration and unified support will be an essential element of the Assistant Director’s role in executing their duties and responsibilities. Responsibilities · Manage and mentor the Coordinator of Facilities & Scheduling and oversee the daily scheduling of campus facilities through the campus reservation software (25Live) and the distribution of event information to campus stakeholders and support staff departments, including: Facilities Management, Information Technology, Campus Police, and Dining Services. · Maintain an expeditious flow of communication on campus as it pertains to event scheduling and event coordination. · In the absence of the Coordinator of Facilities and Scheduling, assume all duties and responsibilities as needed to maintain departmental operational continuity. · Responsible for the maintenance and accuracy of the Campus Scheduling Software and the Campus Events Calendar. · Oversee the execution of all campus event and operational logistics, internal and external to the College. Ensure high-level customer service and support for all events held on Campus by all constituencies. · Develop, maintain, and oversee all external program event planning documents, event itineraries, and program details, and ensure their execution. · Manage, maintain, and oversee departmental summer planning documents, including the summer lodging schedule, energy management schedule, and summer dining schedule. · Foster and maintain close professional relationships with campus support service departments and external clients. · Solicit client feedback while providing immediate follow-up on completed events. Track results and transpose them into metrics for post-event evaluations with campus stakeholders. · Administer an action plan to systematically address customer service issues to provide a means for continuous operational improvement. · Act as a representative of the department and the College in promoting the use of the campus and its facilities to external groups and organizations. · Assist the Associate Director with the administration and management of the departmental marketing plan. · At the discretion of the Associate Director, assist with responding to inquiries by external entities, preparing bid packages and Requests for Proposals, and conducting prospective client site visits showcasing the campus and its amenities. · Provide assistance with client invoicing, accounts receivable tracking, and License Agreement Development. · Hire, train, and supervise full-time and part-time student employees for the Academic Year and Summer Conferencing Season. · Manage departmental key and access control over the summer months, with responsibility for the residential housing key cabinet and access card inventory, allocation, tracking, and replacements. · Maintain and oversee all campus event-related directional and signage endeavors. · Manage the scheduling of event-related vendors for external constituencies and provide oversight of their services. · Oversee and manage the departmental work-study and student employee budget to coincide with departmental strategic initiatives. · Must be able to work evenings and weekends as necessary, in particular during the Summer Conference Season (End of May to Mid/Late August). · Other duties as assigned based on departmental demand. Typical Qualifications · Bachelor’s Degree from an accredited College or University. · 2-4 years of progressive work experience, preferably in a collegiate conference and event services setting. · Excellent communication skills, particularly the ability to persuade, whether verbally or through the written word. · Exceptional organizational skills, including the ability to manage multiple projects and multitask with a high level of detail. · Outstanding interpersonal skills, including independent judgment, the ability to relate well to constituents at all levels of the institution, and to demonstrate to others that they are valued. · Must be a strategic thinker, able to work independently, and must be comfortable with technology. · Ability to work in a fast-paced service environment is essential. · A sense of humor and ability to deal with all situations calmly and pleasantly. · Computer literate; strong Microsoft Office software knowledge; 25Live campus scheduling enterprise familiarity; and desire to adapt technology to best leverage department/divisional/institutional resources. · Must be able to work evenings and weekends as necessary. Supplemental Information The schedule for this position is Monday-Friday, 8:30 am-4:30 pm, with some hours outside that range. Stonehill College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees, based on position, which includes: Medical, prescription drug, vision, and dental coverage Paid vacation, holidays, and various leave programs Competitive retirement benefits, including defined contribution plans and voluntary tax-deferred savings options Employee and dependent educational benefits Life insurance coverage Employee discount programs Voluntray benefits For detailed information on benefits and eligibility, please visit here for more information. Employer Stonehill College (MA) Address 320 Washington Street Easton, Massachusetts, 02357 Phone 508-565-1105 Website www.stonehill.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stonehill/jobs/5466883/assistant-diving-coach": {
      "title": "Assistant Diving Coach",
      "description": "Assistant Diving Coach Salary $750.00 Monthly Location Easton, MA Job Type Admin Non-Benefit Eligible Job Number 00037 Department Athletics Salaries Opening Date 08/31/2026 FLSA Exempt Bargaining Unit N/A Description Benefits Founded by the Congregation of Holy Cross in 1948, Stonehill is a private Catholic college located just 22 miles from downtown Boston on a beautiful 384-acre campus in Easton, Massachusetts. With a student-to-faculty ratio of 13:1, the College engages its 2,500+ students in 80+ rigorous academic programs in the liberal arts, sciences, and pre-professional fields. Faithful to the Holy Cross tradition in education, Stonehill College is committed to developing the moral, spiritual, intellectual, physical, and social competencies of its students and seeks to build and sustain a campus community that embraces diversity and inclusion in its teaching, learning, living, and working. Stonehill values a diversity of persons, opinions, and cultural and religious perspectives. In fulfillment of its motto, Lux et Spes (“Light and Hope”), the College cultivates in its students the competence to think, act, and lead with courage towards creating a more just and compassionate world. Description Position Summary Assists the head coach in the recruitment of perspective student-athletes as well as game-day and practice preparation. Responsibilities Essential Duties and Responsibilities Effectively assist in coaching a competitive diving program in a manner which promotes sportsmanship and integrity, ensuring compliance with all NCAA , Northeast conference, and institutional rules and regulations Communicates effectively and often with the head coach and the student-athletes Establish and maintain good personal relationships with student-athletes and staff Assist in providing a supportive, encouraging, and challenging environment for the student-athletes Provide effective and corrective biomechanical feedback to the team, small groups, and individuals Ability to work weekdays –early mornings and/or early evenings, some weekends Coaching at least 3 practices per week and being available for occasional recruiting Typical Qualifications Education and/or Experience Successful candidate must have a bachelor’s degree and previous swim coaching experience at the high school, club, or NAIA level. Preferred Qualifications Previous collegiate dive coaching experience preferred. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Strong oral and written communication Coaching philosophy and teaching concepts compatible with the Head Coach and Athletic Department’s vision Ability to develop and maintain positive and professional relationships with student-athletes and staff Ability to work effectively with individuals from diverse ethnic, cultural and socioeconomic backgrounds Physical Demands While performing the duties of this Job, the employee is regularly required to sit and talk or hear. The employee is frequently required to use hands to finger, handle, or feel. The employee is occasionally required to stand; walk and reach with hands and arms. The employee must occasionally lift and/or move up to 30 pounds. Specific vision abilities required by this job include close vision, distance vision and ability to adjust focus. Work Environment While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly exposed to an indoor environment that is warm. The employee may get wet. The noise level in the environment is usually moderate but can become loud. Licenses/Certifications Driver’s License Required American Red Cross Lifeguard and first Aid and CPR/AED certification preferred Stonehill College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees, based on position, which includes: Medical, prescription drug, vision, and dental coverage Paid vacation, holidays, and various leave programs Competitive retirement benefits, including defined contribution plans and voluntary tax-deferred savings options Employee and dependent educational benefits Life insurance coverage Employee discount programs Voluntray benefits For detailed information on benefits and eligibility, please visit here for more information. Employer Stonehill College (MA) Address 320 Washington Street Easton, Massachusetts, 02357 Phone 508-565-1105 Website www.stonehill.edu Apply"
